             [--MM _b_a_c_k_e_x_t] [--oo _o_u_t_f_i_l_e] [--xx 00 | 11 | 22] _f_i_l_e _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The uunniiffddeeff utility selectively processes conditional cpp(1) directives.
     It removes from a file both the directives and any additional text that
     they specify should be removed, while otherwise leaving the file alone.

     The uunniiffddeeff utility acts on ##iiff, ##iiffddeeff, ##iiffnnddeeff, ##eelliiff, ##eellssee, and
     ##eennddiiff lines, using macros specified in --DD and --UU command line options or
     in --ff definition files.  A directive is processed if the macro specifica‐
     tions are sufficient to provide a definite value for its control expres‐
     sion.  If the result is false, the directive and the following lines un‐
     der its control are removed.  If the result is true, only the directive
     is removed.  An ##iiffddeeff or ##iiffnnddeeff directive is passed through unchanged
     if its controlling macro is not specified.  Any ##iiff or ##eelliiff control ex‐
     pression that has an unknown value or that uunniiffddeeff cannot parse is passed
     through unchanged.  By default, uunniiffddeeff ignores ##iiff and ##eelliiff lines with
     constant expressions; it can be told to process them by specifying the --kk
     flag on the command line.

     It understands a commonly-used subset of the expression syntax for ##iiff
     and ##eelliiff lines: integer constants, integer values of macros defined on
     the command line, the ddeeffiinneedd() operator, the operators !!, <<, >>, <<==, >>==,
     ====, !!==, &&&&, ||||, and parenthesized expressions.  A kind of “short circuit”
     evaluation is used for the &&&& operator: if either operand is definitely
     false then the result is false, even if the value of the other operand is
     unknown.  Similarly, if either operand of |||| is definitely true then the
     result is true.

     When evaluating an expression, uunniiffddeeff does not expand macros first.  The
     value of a macro must be a simple number, not an expression.  A limited
     form of indirection is allowed, where one macro's value is the name of
     another.

     In most cases, uunniiffddeeff does not distinguish between object-like macros
     (without arguments) and function-like macros (with arguments).  A func‐
     tion-like macro invocation can appear in ##iiff and ##eelliiff control expres‐
     sions.  If the macro is not explicitly defined, or is defined with the --DD
     flag on the command-line, or with ##ddeeffiinnee in a --ff definition file, its
     arguments are ignored.  If a macro is explicitly undefined on the command
     line with the --UU flag, or with ##uunnddeeff in a --ff definition file, it may not
     have any arguments since this leads to a syntax error.

     The uunniiffddeeff utility understands just enough about C to know when one of
     the directives is inactive because it is inside a comment, or affected by
     a backslash-continued line.  It spots unusually-formatted preprocessor
     directives and knows when the layout is too odd for it to handle.

     The options are as follows:

     --BB      Compress blank lines around a deleted section.  Mutually exclu‐
             sive with the --bb option.

     --bb      Replace removed lines with blank lines instead of deleting them.
             Mutually exclusive with the --BB option.

     --cc      Complement.  That is, lines that would have been removed or
             blanked are retained and vice versa.

     --DD_s_y_m[=_v_a_l]
     --UU_s_y_m   Specify that a macro is defined (--DD) to a given value, to 1 if no
             value is given, or undefined (--UU).

             If the same macro appears in more than one argument, the last oc‐
             currence dominates.

     --dd      Turn on printing of debugging messages.

     --ee      By default, uunniiffddeeff will report an error if it needs to remove a
             preprocessor directive that spans more than one line, for exam‐
             ple, if it has a multi-line comment hanging off its right hand
             end.  The --ee flag makes it ignore the line instead.

     --ff _d_e_f_i_l_e
             The file _d_e_f_i_l_e contains ##ddeeffiinnee and ##uunnddeeff preprocessor direc‐
             tives, which have the same effect as the corresponding --DD and --UU
             command line arguments.  Multiple --ff arguments can be given and
             mixed with --DD and --UU arguments; later options override earlier
             ones.

             Each directive must be on a single line.  Object-like macro defi‐
             nitions (without arguments) are set to the given value.  Func‐
             tion-like macro definitions (with arguments) are treated as if
             they are set to 1.

     --iiDD_s_y_m[=_v_a_l]
     --iiUU_s_y_m  Ignore ##iiffddeeffs.  If C code uses ##iiffddeeffs to delimit non-C lines,
             such as comments or code which is under construction, this tells
             uunniiffddeeff which symbols are used for that purpose so that it will
             not try to parse comments and line continuations inside those
             ##iiffddeeffs.

             If the same macro appears in more than one argument, the last oc‐
             currence dominates.

     --hh      Print help.

     --KK      Always treat the result of &&&& and |||| operators as unknown if ei‐
             ther operand is unknown, instead of short-circuiting when unknown
             operands can't affect the result.  This option is for compatibil‐
             ity with older versions of uunniiffddeeff.

     --kk      Process ##iiff and ##eelliiff lines with constant expressions.  By de‐
             fault, sections controlled by such lines are passed through un‐
             changed because they typically start “#if 0” and are used as a
             kind of comment to sketch out future or past development.  It
             would be rude to strip them out, just as it would be for normal
             comments.

     --MM _b_a_c_k_e_x_t
             Modify input files in place, and keep backups of the original
             files by appending the _b_a_c_k_e_x_t to the input filenames.  If a zero
             length _b_a_c_k_e_x_t is given, no backup will be saved.

     --mm      Modify one or more input files in place.

     --nn      Add #line directives to the output following any deleted lines,
             so that errors produced when compiling the output file correspond
             to line numbers in the input file.

     --oo _o_u_t_f_i_l_e
             Write output to the file _o_u_t_f_i_l_e instead of the standard output
             when processing a single file.

     --SS      Like the --ss option, but the nesting depth of each macro is also
             printed.  This is useful for working out the number of possible
             combinations of interdependent defined/undefined macros.

     --ss      Instead of processing an input file as usual, this option causes
             uunniiffddeeff to produce a list of macros that are used in preprocessor
             directive controlling expressions.

     --tt      Disables parsing for C strings, comments, and line continuations,
             which is useful for plain text.  This is a blanket version of the
             --iiDD and --iiUU flags.

     --VV      Print version details.

     --xx 00 | 11 | 22
             Set exit status mode to zero, one, or two.  See the _E_X_I_T _S_T_A_T_U_S
             section below for details.

     The uunniiffddeeff utility takes its input from _s_t_d_i_n if there are no _f_i_l_e argu‐
     ments.  The --mm or --MM options must be used if there are multiple input
     files.  A dash (-) specifies input from stdin or output to stdout.

     The uunniiffddeeff utility works nicely with the --DD option of diff(1).

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     In normal usage the uunniiffddeeff utility's exit status depends on the mode set
     using the --xx option.

     If the exit mode is zero (the default) then uunniiffddeeff exits with status 0
     if the output is an exact copy of the input, or with status 1 if the out‐
     put differs.

     If the exit mode is one, uunniiffddeeff exits with status 1 if the output is un‐
     modified or 0 if it differs.

     If the exit mode is two, uunniiffddeeff exits with status zero in both cases.

     In all exit modes, uunniiffddeeff exits with status 2 if there is an error.

     The exit status is 0 if the --hh or --VV command line options are given.

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     EOF in comment

     Inappropriate ##eelliiff, ##eellssee or ##eennddiiff

     Obfuscated preprocessor control line

     Premature EOF (with the line number of the most recent unterminated ##iiff)

     Too many levels of nesting

